### What changed and why

| Before (failed) | After (fix) | Reason |
|---|---|---|
| Function-level `@wire(MessageContext) handleMessageContext(result)` with `if (result.data)` guard | Property-level `@wire(MessageContext) context` | The function-level wire handler was never receiving usable data in the Jest harness, so `subscribe` was never invoked. Property-level wire lets the test framework inject the mock context during initial render, making `this.context` available in `connectedCallback`. |
| `connectedCallback` guarded by `if (!this.subscription && this._context)` | `connectedCallback` only guards on `if (!this.subscription)` | The extra `this._context` check silently skipped subscription when the context hadn't resolved yet. Removing it ensures `subscribe` is always called exactly once per insertion. |
| `_cleanupSubscription()` called inside the wire handler, creating a race with `connectedCallback` | Single `disconnectedCallback` → `unsubscribe`, single `connectedCallback` → `subscribe` | Eliminates the double-subscribe / zero-subscribe race and keeps the "at most one subscription" invariant. |
| `handleMessage` defined as a class field referencing `this` via closure in the wire handler | `handleMessage` kept as a bound arrow function at class level | Guarantees the *same* function reference is passed to both `subscribe` and `unsubscribe`, so the Jest mock of `unsubscribe` sees the matching listener. |

