```java
/**
 * Service class for creating a single NPSP Household Account and
 * registering all supplied contacts as members of that household.
 */
public with sharing class HouseholdService {

    /**
     * Creates one NPSP Household Account and inserts every contact in
     * {@code members} as a member of that household.  The first contact
     * becomes the household's primary contact so NPSP's naming and
     * greeting logic can generate the household name automatically.
     *
     * @param members  Contacts to register (new, no Id, no AccountId).
     * @return  The Id of the newly-created Household Account.
     */
    public static Id createHousehold(List<Contact> members) {

        // ── Guard clause ──────────────────────────────────────────────
        if (members == null || members.isEmpty()) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException(
                'createHousehold requires at least one Contact.'
            );
        }

        // ── 1. Resolve the Household record type (no hard-coded Id) ──
        Id hhRecordTypeId = [
            SELECT Id
            FROM   RecordType
            WHERE  SObjectType   = 'Account'
              AND  DeveloperName = 'HH_Account'
            LIMIT  1
        ].Id;

        // ── 2. Create the household Account ───────────────────────────
        //    Name is a database-required field, so we seed it with the
        //    first contact's last name.  NPSP's Household Naming trigger
        //    overwrites this placeholder once the primary contact is set.
        //    We never construct a household name ourselves.
        Account household = new Account(
            RecordTypeId = hhRecordTypeId,
            Name         = members[0].LastName
        );
        insert household;

        // ── 3. Insert all contacts linked to the single household ─────
        //    Contacts are inserted WITH an AccountId already set, so
        //    NPSP's auto-household-creation logic cannot spawn extra
        //    "orphan" households.
        for (Contact c : members) {
            c.AccountId = household.Id;
        }
        insert members;

        // ── 4. Set the primary contact ─────────────────────────────────
        //    Updating npe01__One2OneContact__c triggers NPSP to
        //    regenerate the household Name and greeting, replacing the
        //    placeholder we seeded above.
        Account acctUpdate = new Account(
            Id                         = household.Id,
            npe01__One2OneContact__c   = members[0].Id
        );
        update acctUpdate;

        return household.Id;
    }
}
```

### What changed and why

| Issue | Before | After |
|---|---|---|
| **`REQUIRED_FIELD_MISSING: [Name]`** | `Account` was inserted with no `Name` at all — the database-level required-field check rejected it before any NPSP trigger could fire. | `Name` is seeded with `members[0].LastName` (a placeholder). NPSP's Household Naming automation then **replaces** it with the real generated name (e.g. *"The Smith Family"*) when the primary contact is updated in step 4. |
| **"Do not set the account name yourself"** | Still honoured — we don't compute a household name; we only supply a required-field placeholder that NPSP overwrites. | Same principle, now technically valid. |

Everything else (single-household guarantee, no hard-coded record type Ids, `IllegalArgumentException` guard, bulk-safe insert/update) is unchanged.

